\section*{1. Introduction}
The Continuous Yield Propulsion Core (CYPC-1) is a high-stability propulsion
module derived from the ACE-9K conservation engine. This chapter defines the
integration of CYPC-1 into a full spacecraft architecture, including structural
mounting, thermal routing, thrust-vector control, manifold stability coupling,
and conservation-layer synchronization.

\section*{2. Spacecraft-Level Architecture Overview}
CYPC-1 integrates into the spacecraft through five primary interfaces:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Structural Interface Frame (SIF)
    \item Thermal Regulation Network (TRN)
    \item Thrust Vector Control Grid (TVCG)
    \item Stability Manifold Coupling Layer (SMCL)
    \item Conservation Synchronization Bus (CSB)
\end{enumerate}

These interfaces ensure CYPC-1 operates within global spacecraft constraints
while maintaining continuous-yield thrust stability.

\section*{3. Structural Interface Frame (SIF)}
\subsection*{3.1 Structural Coupling}
CYPC-1 mounts to the spacecraft via a composite frame designed to maintain:
\[
f_{\text{yield}} = \sqrt{\sigma_{ij}\sigma^{ij}} - \sigma_{\text{crit}} < 0.
\]

\subsection*{3.2 Requirements}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Multi-axis load distribution
    \item Strain-sensor lattice for real-time deformation mapping
    \item Nozzle alignment tolerance $< 0.002^\circ$
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{3.3 Integration Notes}
The SIF must ensure that thrust-induced deformation does not propagate into
critical spacecraft subsystems.

\section*{4. Thermal Regulation Network (TRN)}
\subsection*{4.1 Thermal Model}
Thermal flux from CYPC-1 is governed by:
\[
Q = \int P(x)\, dV,
\]
where $P(x)$ is the information-density pressure field.

\subsection*{4.2 Requirements}
\begin{itemize}
    \item High-capacity thermal routing channels
    \item Plasma exhaust heat dissipation manifold
    \item Temperature stability envelope $< 0.5^\circ$ variation
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{4.3 Integration Notes}
TRN must prevent thermal feedback into QP-Core gradient coils.

\section*{5. Thrust Vector Control Grid (TVCG)}
\subsection*{5.1 Governing Dynamics}
Thrust vectoring is defined by:
\[
M\ddot{x}^\mu + D\dot{x}^\mu + Kx^\mu = F^\mu.
\]

\subsection*{5.2 Requirements}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Magnetic-plasma nozzle field coils
    \item Dynamic stiffness modulation ($K$-matrix control)
    \item Damping matrix $D$ maintained positive definite
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{5.3 Integration Notes}
TVCG interfaces with spacecraft attitude control systems for coordinated
maneuvering.

\section*{6. Stability Manifold Coupling Layer (SMCL)}
\subsection*{6.1 Governing Stability Condition}
Global stability requires:
\[
\lambda_{\max}(K - \Gamma_P) < 0.
\]

\subsection*{6.2 Requirements}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Real-time eigenvalue spectrum evaluation
    \item Geometry modulation actuators
    \item Nozzle deformation sensors
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{6.3 Integration Notes}
SMCL ensures spacecraft-level structural modes do not destabilize CYPC-1’s
manifold.

\section*{7. Conservation Synchronization Bus (CSB)}
\subsection*{7.1 Governing Conservation Laws}
CYPC-1 synchronizes with spacecraft systems to maintain:
\[
\nabla_\mu T^{\mu\nu} = 0, \qquad
\nabla_\mu J^\mu = 0,
\]
and preserves:
\[
\frac{dH_{\text{metric}}}{dt} \approx 0.
\]

\subsection*{7.2 Requirements}
\begin{itemize}
    \item High-bandwidth conservation telemetry bus
    \item Cross-subsystem divergence monitoring
    \item Invariant preservation feedback loop
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{7.3 Integration Notes}
CSB ensures spacecraft-wide conservation integrity during thrust cycles.

\section*{8. Full Spacecraft Integration Pipeline}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Mount CYPC-1 to SIF and validate structural yield envelope.
    \item Connect TRN and verify thermal routing under plasma compression.
    \item Integrate TVCG for thrust vector modulation.
    \item Synchronize SMCL for global manifold stability.
    \item Activate CSB for conservation and invariant monitoring.
\end{enumerate}

\section*{9. Stability Envelope}
Spacecraft-level stability requires:
\[
\lambda_{\max}(K - \Gamma_P) < 0,
\]
with propulsion-cycle value:
\[
\lambda_{\max} = -0.387.
\]

\section*{10. Integration Notes}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Spacecraft structural modes must not interfere with CYPC-1 eigenvalue spectrum.
    \item Thermal routing must prevent feedback into QP-Core gradient coils.
    \item Conservation synchronization must remain continuous during thrust vectoring.
\end{itemize}

\section*{11. Conclusion}
CYPC-1 integrates into spacecraft architecture through structural, thermal,
dynamic, stability, and conservation interfaces. The resulting propulsion system
maintains continuous-yield thrust generation with global manifold stability and
zero conservation drift.

